# Dashboard Builder Guide

<rule>
<meta>
  Style conventions, proven patterns, and checklists for building A7S Skipper dashboards.
  This is the go-to reference for authoring best practices. For schema details, see the specific widget rules.
</meta>

<layout>
  <grid_system>
    **Always prefer section-level `layout: horizontal` + `columns: N`** for multi-column layouts.

    ```yaml
    # GOOD — section controls layout
    sections:
      - id: kpi-cards
        layout: horizontal
        columns: 3
        widgets:
          - id: revenue-card
            type: markdown
          - id: costs-card
            type: markdown
          - id: roi-card
            type: markdown

    # BAD — col_span on each widget
    sections:
      - id: kpi-cards
        widgets:
          - id: revenue-card
            type: markdown
            col_span: 4                    # Unnecessary
          - id: costs-card
            type: markdown
            col_span: 4
          - id: roi-card
            type: markdown
            col_span: 4
    ```

    Reserve `col_span` for: unequal widths, wide tables (`col_span: 15`).
    Reserve `row_span` for: multi-track charts needing extra height (`row_span: 2+`).
  </grid_system>
</layout>

<patterns>
  <root_structure>
    ```text
    dashboards/<root>/
      +layout.yaml                    # name, icon, order, default_dashboard
      _context.yaml                   # AI roles, rules, glossary
      _changelog.yaml                 # Product changelog
      _shared/
        _filters.yaml                 # Inherited by all dashboards
        _mappings.yaml                # Value mappings
        _datasources.yaml             # Shared datasources
      01-dashboard-name/
        _tab.yaml                     # Meta only (id, title, icon, description, ai)
        _shared/
          _datasources.yaml
          _filters.yaml
        01-overview.yaml
        02-by-source.yaml
    ```
  </root_structure>

  <tab_hierarchy>
    Structure tabs from high-level to granular:
    1. **Overview** — daily aggregated, no grouping
    2. **By dimension** — grouped by one dimension (media source, language)
    3. **By multiple** — grouped by 2–3 dimensions
    4. **Charts** — loss analysis, cohort, YoY comparison
  </tab_hierarchy>

  <naming_conventions>
    | Element | Convention | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Dashboard name | Human-readable, 2–4 words | "Marketing Performance" |
    | Dashboard ID | `snake_case` | `marketing_performance` |
    | Tab title | 1–3 words | "Overview", "By Channel" |
    | Section ID | `snake_case` | `key_metrics`, `trend_analysis` |
    | Widget ID | `kebab-case` | `daily-costs-table`, `revenue-chart` |
    | Datasource key | `snake_case` | `daily_marketing`, `by_media_source` |
    | All titles | Human-readable, capitalized | Never technical slugs |
  </naming_conventions>

  <sql_conventions>
    ```sql
    SELECT toDate(install_date) AS date
         , SUM(costs) AS costs
         , SUM(revenue_net) AS revenue_net
    FROM marts.marketing_creatives
    WHERE ${filters.date_range('toDate(install_date)')}
    GROUP BY date
    ORDER BY date DESC
    ```

    - Leading commas for column list
    - `${filters.<id>('col')}` for ClickHouse-side filtering
    - `GROUP BY` by column name, not ordinal
    - `ORDER BY` primary metric `DESC`
    - No trailing semicolons
    - Alias all expressions (`SUM(costs) AS costs`)
    - Always guard division: `NULLIF(denominator, 0)`
  </sql_conventions>

  <ai_metadata>
    Every dashboard should have a rich AI block:

    ```yaml
    ai:
      roles:
        preferred: buyer
        extra: [owner, marketing_analyst]
      dataset:
        name: Marketing Performance
        description: >
          Daily marketing data: spend, revenue, ROI, conversions
          by media source, language, campaign, adset, ad.
        language: en
        date_column: date
        dimensions: [date, media_source, language, campaign]
        metrics:
          costs: SUM(costs)
          revenue_net: SUM(revenue_net)
          roi_net: (SUM(revenue_net) - SUM(costs)) / NULLIF(SUM(costs), 0) * 100
        business:
          rules:
            - "ROI Net below -45% is critical."
      welcome: "Ask about marketing spend, revenue, and ROI."
      examples: ["What is the total spend for the last 7 days?"]
    ```

    Checklist:
    - `roles.preferred` + `roles.extra` from root `_context.yaml`
    - `dataset.name` — short name
    - `dataset.description` — one paragraph with all dimensions and metrics
    - `dataset.date_column` — primary time column
    - `dataset.dimensions` + `dataset.metrics` — enumerate all
    - `dataset.business.rules` — thresholds, conventions
    - `welcome` / `examples` in both EN and RU
  </ai_metadata>

  <ai_hints>
    Add `ai_hint` to datasources, sections, and widgets:
    ```yaml
    datasources:
      daily_marketing:
        ai_hint: "Daily aggregated marketing metrics. Pre-grouped by date."
    sections:
      - id: overall
        ai_hint: "Daily time-series — one row per date."
        widgets:
          - id: daily-costs-table
            ai_hint: "Main overview table with costs, revenue, ROI."
    ```
  </ai_hints>

  <rich_table_standards>
    Always define:
    - `column_order` explicitly
    - `visualization: { zebra: true, zebra_rows: odd }`
    - `sorting: { enabled: true, default: [{ id: costs, desc: true }] }`
    - `export: { enabled: true, formats: [csv] }`
    - Conditional formatting on ROI columns

    Formatting best practices:
    - Omit `decimals` from column format to get adaptive auto-detection from data
    - Use `steps: auto` for simple color scales — auto-generates gradient from data percentiles
    - Choose palette from design system: diverging (`red_green`, `blue_orange`, `purple_teal`, `red_yellow_green`) or sequential (`green`, `blue`, `purple`, `orange`, `gray`)
    - Use `auto_hide: true` on optional columns that may be empty — hides when 100% null (opt-in, disabled by default)

    Standard ROI color scale:
    ```yaml
    conditional_formatting:
      - column: roi_net
        type: color_scale
        value_sql: |
          CASE
            WHEN costs > 2000 AND roi_net < -45 THEN -100
            WHEN roi_net > 0 THEN 100
            ELSE roi_net
          END
        steps:
          - { value: -45, color: "#F7A2A2" }
          - { value: -44.99, color: "#ffffff" }
          - { value: 0, color: "#ffffff" }
          - { value: 0.01, color: "#91E2AF" }
    ```

    Auto color scale (for simple metrics):
    ```yaml
    conditional_formatting:
      - column: revenue
        type: color_scale
        palette: red_yellow_green
        steps: auto
    ```
  </rich_table_standards>

  <chart_patterns>
    | Pattern | Preset | Key config |
    |---|---|---|
    | Time series | `trend` | `series: [{ metric, type: line }]` |
    | YoY comparison | `comparison` | `drill_down: { columns: [year, month, day] }` |
    | Category bars | `breakdown` | `sort: value_desc` |
    | Profit/loss | `loss_analysis` | horizontal bars |
    | Cohort curves | `cohort` | `series: [{ metric, type: line }]` |
    | Stacked panels | `multi_track` | `row_span: 2`, series as tracks |
  </chart_patterns>

  <shared_filters>
    Standard date range filter (root-level `_shared/_filters.yaml`):
    ```yaml
    - id: date_range
      type: date_range
      column: install_date
      presets:
        - { id: today, label: Today, tag: Live }
        - { id: yesterday, label: Yesterday, tag: Live }
        - { id: last_7_days, label: "Last 7 days", tag: Rolling }
        - { id: last_14_days, label: "Last 14 days", tag: Rolling }
        - { id: last_30_days, label: "Last 30 days", tag: Rolling }
        - { id: this_month, label: This month }
        - { id: last_month, label: Last month }
      default:
        preset: last_14_days
    ```
  </shared_filters>

  <design_system>
    - Use canonical chart palettes: `default`, `ocean`, `sunset`
    - Prefer named design-system colors over custom hex values
    - Semantic colors (success/warning/error/info) for status only
    - If MCP available, inspect `skipper://design-system` and `skipper://design-system/palettes`
  </design_system>
</patterns>

<rules>
  <checklist>
    Before delivering a dashboard:
    - [ ] Every datasource has `ai_hint`
    - [ ] Every section has `ai_hint`
    - [ ] Dashboard has rich `ai` metadata block
    - [ ] Filter macros used (no hardcoded dates)
    - [ ] `{{source:key}}` references (no hardcoded table names)
    - [ ] Rich tables: `column_order`, columns, `zebra: true`, sorting, export
    - [ ] Charts use appropriate presets
    - [ ] SQL validated via `validate_sql`
    - [ ] Dashboard validated via `validate_dashboard`
    - [ ] Design-system palettes used
  </checklist>

  <mcp_exploration>
    `preview_query` and `explore_metric` deliver results in three channels:

    | Channel | Rows | Format | Purpose |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | `content[0].text` | ≤ 500 | JSON | LLM context |
    | `structuredContent` | ≤ 1,000 | JSON table | UI clients (zero tokens) |
    | `resource_link` | ≤ 10,000 | CSV | Full data via `skipper://query-results/{id}` |

    `explore_metric` supports:
    - `segment_by` — string or array for multi-dimension grouping
    - `filters` — `[{ dimension, operator, values }]` with `eq`, `neq`, `in`, `not_in`
  </mcp_exploration>
</rules>

<keyboard_shortcuts>
  Dashboards support a comprehensive keyboard-first navigation experience:

  **Global shortcuts** (available on any page):
  | Shortcut | Action |
  |----------|--------|
  | `⌘K` / `Ctrl+K` | Open command palette — search dashboards, tabs, actions |
  | `?` | Show keyboard shortcuts reference |
  | `r` | Refresh all datasources on current dashboard |
  | `f` | Focus the filter panel |
  | `t` | Toggle light/dark theme |
  | `d` | Cycle density (compact → comfortable → spacious) |
  | `Alt+1..9` | Switch to tab N on multi-tab dashboards |

  **Command palette modes:**
  | Prefix | Mode |
  |--------|------|
  | (none) | Search dashboards, starred, recent |
  | `>` | Commands only (refresh, theme, density, devtools) |
  | `#` | Search within current dashboard (tabs, sections, widgets) |

  **Table shortcuts** (when a rich_table widget has focus):
  | Key | Action |
  |-----|--------|
  | `↑` / `↓` | Navigate rows |
  | `Enter` | Select row |
  | `Space` | Toggle checkbox (when selectable) |
  | `Home` / `End` | First / last row |
  | `Page Up/Down` | Jump by viewport height |
  | `s` | Focus SmartSearch (when `global_search` enabled) |
</keyboard_shortcuts>

